Defining the Problem 
We are designing a real time system that matches riders with the nearest available drivers in a city , optimized for low latency and scalability.

Functional Requirements :-
Rider can request a ride.
System finds nearby available drivers.
Driver can accept / reject.
Rider get driver details.
Update driver and rider locations in real time.

Optional (mention only if asked) :- like cancellation , Timeout if no driver accepts , Retry matching.

Non-Functional Requirements. (good way to mention CAP)
Low Latency
High Availability (no downtime)
Scalable (millions of users)
Consistent location updates 
Fault Tolerant.

Availability is prioritized over strong consistency.In ride matching case , it is more important that the system responds quickly even if location data is slightly
stlale , so eventual consistency is acceptable.

1)Ride Matching Part.
2)Location Handling.

So, in Ride Matching Part , first Rider request for a ride , after this , the system checks and finds top N drivers which are available. And after that , whoever
driver confirms the ride , the driver is locked to prevent double booking.Driver location is stored in redis using GeoHash , which allows fast radius-based queries.

The main challenge in location handling is to frequently update millions of driver locations and efficiently query nearby drivers with very low latency.
So, for storing this , Redis is used. 
How Geo-Spatial indexing works ?
1) Convert Location to GeoHash String -> (latitude + longitude ) -> GeoHash String
2) Nearby locations share the same prefix.
this is what makes searching faster.
So, when you want drivers near a rider , first compute rider's geohash , then drivers with same prefixes are searched .

How Geo-Spatial indexing in Redis works ?
So, first converts location to number (GeoHash). This is called GeoHash. Then , Redis uses a sorted set (ZSET). Redis converts location to geoscore.
Redis looks for scores closer to that number. It only scans nearby entries.

Ques. How does GeoHash approximte distance.
GeoHash does not calculate distance. It partitions the earth into grid cells and helps quickly identify nearby drivers. Exact distance is then computed.
Computation of distance is expensive.

3)Handling timeouts 
If no driver accepts the ride within a timeout window , the system retries matching with the next set of drivers or expands the search radius.
If a driver app crashes or disconnects , the lock is automatically released.


Scaling the system and all other important stuff like availability , durability ,Reliability etc...
Ques. Why is slight inaccuracy acceptable?
Slight inaccuracy is acceptable because geo spatial indexing is only used for fast candidate selection. Exact distance and routing are computed later, and in ride
matching , speed and availability are more important than centimeter level precision.

Ques. GeoHash vs QuadTree.
QuadTrees are useful when the data is static like map. QuadTree gives better precison but geohash offers better performance and simplicity. Quadtrees divide the
whole map recursively into 4 regions. 

Ques. Why Uber moved to H3 ? (only mention , if asked)
because in geospatial, the grid is divided into squares , wheres in H3 , the area is divided into hexagonal grids , which reduces the boundary distortion or double
counting the riders or missing the riders. Radius-based queries can be done easily here.

Databases prioritize durability , redis prioritizes speed . Live Location data needs speed. Traditional database stores the informatino about the driver and rider 
data.

Ques. What if Redis crashes ?
Solution : Replication means keepung a copy of Redis data on another machine. and Redis Sentinel is like a watchman which watches Redis servers and automatically
switches to a Replica if the master fails.

Ques. How many neighboring cells are checked ?
We check the current cell and a small number of neighboring cells , expanding outward until enough candidate drivers are found.

Ques. How traffic and ETA override distance ?
ETA basically means estimated time of arrival , and this is more preffered than distance , that's why after generating the candidate , and computing the distance
, neighbors are ranked according to ETA for better customer experience which determines how easily a driver can reach the destination.

to make it scalable -> using redis (in memory cache) , and using load balancer.
to make it available every time -> not using single point of failure , like horizontal scaling and rerouting when required
minimum latency -> using sorted set , zset , using geospatial indexing for finding the nearby drivers. 
performance -> minimum latency , improves the performance.

Revision:-
Functional requirements :- Driver's location should be updated every time , so that the rider knows where the driver is . User should be able to book or cancel the ride.
System should be able to make correct match driver with rider.

Non functional requirements :- scalibility , availability , low latency , fault tolerance.

so, driver's location needs to be updated within every 5 seconds and websocket is created with the server , such that the location (long,lat) is sent to the server,
and then the server , sents it to the function which converts it to geohash , and then it is then stored in the dataset.
now , when user wants to book a ride , first the word with the same prefixes are find , to find the approximate distance of drivers , it is stored in redis cache
, because it can be accessed more easily and has minimum latency , and then if that driver accepts it , then it is proceeded otherwise , the next driver is called.

but , we have edge problem with geohash , because it divides the region into squares , and if a driver is in the edge , then for the both the strings for the different
squares even with the same distance , it will have completely different prefix , so to solve this problem h3 is proposed , which divides the region into hexagons.
such that first server converts the (long,lat) to hexagonal cell, and that hexagonal cell is sent to the server , now that server sends it to redis pub/sub model,
where it publishes the updated location of the driver to all the riders which are present in that hexagon , and after that when any rider opens the application , then
it is shown the updated location.

what if driver's internet is patchy ?
then , the updates are stored locally , and then it is send in batch wise , when the driver returns , but if the driver remains offline for more than 30 seconds,
then the status of it is turned offline and it is not assigned any rides.













